If this is an emergency or someone’s health or safety is at immediate risk, call your local emergency number now. In the United States you can also contact SAMHSA’s National Helpline at 1‑800‑662‑HELP (4357) or visit findtreatment.gov for local treatment resources. Would you like information on health effects, signs of addiction, or help locating treatment resources in your area?", "", "", "", "Here’s a concise C# example demonstrating dependency injection (constructor injection) using interfaces and the Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection container. It shows production code and a simple test-friendly fake implementation.

// Interfaces and implementations
```csharp
public interface ILogger
{
    void Log(string message);
}

public class ConsoleLogger : ILogger
{
    public void Log(string message) => Console.WriteLine(message);
}

public interface IRepository
{
    string GetData();
}

public class Repository : IRepository
{
    readonly ILogger _logger;
    public Repository(ILogger logger)
    {
        _logger = logger; // dependency injected
    }

    public string GetData()
    {
        _logger.Log("Fetching data...");
        return "some data";
    }
}

public class ReportGenerator
{
    readonly IRepository _repo;
    readonly ILogger _logger;

    public ReportGenerator(IRepository repo, ILogger logger)
    {
        _repo = repo; // injected
        _logger = logger; // injected
    }

    public void Generate()
    {
        var data = _repo.GetData();
        _logger.Log($"Generating report from: {data}");
    }
}
```

// Registering dependencies and resolving
```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;

var services = new ServiceCollection();
services.AddSingleton<ILogger, ConsoleLogger>();
services.AddTransient<IRepository, Repository>();
services.AddTransient<ReportGenerator>();

using var provider = services.BuildServiceProvider();
var generator = provider.GetRequiredService<ReportGenerator>();
generator.Generate();
```

// Unit test / test double example (no DI container required)
```csharp
public class FakeLogger : ILogger
{
    public List<string> Messages = new();
    public void Log(string message) => Messages.Add(message);
}

// In a test:
var fakeLogger = new FakeLogger();
var repo = new Repository(fakeLogger);
var generator = new ReportGenerator(repo, fakeLogger);

generator.Generate();
// assert fakeLogger.Messages contains expected entries
```

Notes:
- Constructor injection (shown) is the most common and preferred form: dependencies are explicit and immutable.
- Using interfaces makes components loosely coupled and easy to test by supplying fakes/mocks.
- You can also use property (setter) injection or a DI container to manage lifetime and wiring, depending on needs."]

