{"role": "assistant", "content": "Good question. The Network.ResourceType values that imply there will likely be a response body are:

- Document - This represents an HTML document, so it will have HTML in the body.
- Stylesheet - This represents a CSS stylesheet, so it will have CSS in the body. 
- Image - This represents an image, so it will have the raw image data in the body.
- Media - This represents an audio/video resource, so it will likely have media data in the body. 
- Font - This represents a font, so it will have the font data in the body.
- Script - This represents a JavaScript file, so it will have JS code in the body. 
- TextTrack - This represents a text track for media, so it may have subtitle data in the body.
- ApplicationCache - This represents an application cache manifest, so it may have cache data in the body.
- XHR - This represents an XMLHttpRequest, so it may have response data in the body.

So in general, any resource type that represents a document, file, or data of some kind (rather than a redirect or metadata) implies there could be a useful response body.

The types that typically imply an empty or no body are:

- Other 
- Manifest 
- Ping 
- CSPReport 
- Preflight 
- PluginResource

So in your code, you could check the resourceType and assume there will be no body for those types, and a likely body for the other types I mentioned. Let me know if you have any other questions!"},
