Notes:
- The Totals Row is part of the ListObject and is not included when you sort the table.
- When the table is filtered, the totals update correctly (they use the table aggregation behavior).

If you prefer a formula-based total (outside or independent of the table):
- Place a row directly below the table and use SUBTOTAL to sum the table column, e.g.:
  =SUBTOTAL(109, Table1[Amount])
  (109 = SUM for visible cells only — useful if you use filters)

VBA: add or configure the Totals Row programmatically

Example macro (change the table name and column names as needed):
Sub AddTotalsRow()
    Dim lo As ListObject
    Set lo = ActiveSheet.ListObjects("Table1")   ' change Table1 to your table name

    lo.ShowTotals = True
    lo.ListColumns("Amount").TotalsCalculation = xlTotalsCalculationSum
    lo.ListColumns("Quantity").TotalsCalculation = xlTotalsCalculationSum

    ' Put a label in the first totals cell (optional)
    lo.TotalsRowRange.Cells(1, 1).Value = "Totals"
End Sub

