predicted_preference:
["", "", "Here’s a simple, practical walkthrough (with code) showing how to connect a frontend and backend using a REST API.

Steps (high level):
- Design your API endpoints (resources + HTTP methods). Example: GET /api/todos, POST /api/todos.
- Implement the backend that exposes those endpoints and returns/accepts JSON.
- Enable CORS (or serve the frontend from the same origin) so the browser can call the API.
- From the frontend, call the endpoints using fetch or Axios, send/receive JSON, and update the UI.

Backend (Node.js + Express) — save as index.js and run with node:

```javascript
const express = require('express');
const cors = require('cors');
const app = express();

app.use(cors()); // allow browser requests from other origins in dev
app.use(express.json()); // parse JSON bodies

let todos = [ { id: 1, text: 'Buy milk' } ];

app.get('/api/todos', (req, res) => {
  res.json(todos);
});

app.post('/api/todos', (req, res) => {
  const { text } = req.body;
  if (!text) return res.status(400).json({ error: 'text required' });
  const newTodo = { id: Date.now(), text };
  todos.push(newTodo);
  res.status(201).json(newTodo);
});

app.listen(3000, () => console.log('API server listening on http://localhost:3000'));
```

Install/run:
- npm init -y
- npm install express cors
- node index.js

Frontend (static HTML + fetch) — open this file in the browser (or serve it):

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
  <body>
    <ul id="list"></ul>
    <form id="form">
      <input id="text" placeholder="New todo" />
      <button>Add</button>
    </form>

    <script>
      const list = document.getElementById('list');
      const form = document.getElementById('form');
      const input = document.getElementById('text');

      async function load() {
        const res = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/api/todos');
        const data = await res.json();
        list.innerHTML = data.map(t => `<li>${t.text}</li>`).join('');
      }

      form.addEventListener('submit', async (e) => {
        e.preventDefault();
        const res = await fetch('http://localhost:3000/api/todos', {
          method: 'POST',
          headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' },
          body: JSON.stringify({ text: input.value })
        });
        if (res.ok) {
          input.value = '';
          load();
        } else {
          console.error('Failed to add');
        }
      });

      load();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
```

Quick curl examples:
- Get todos: curl http://localhost:3000/api/todos
- Create todo: curl -X POST -H "Content-Type: application/json" -d '{"text":"Walk dog"}' http://localhost:3000/api/todos

Notes and best practices:
- CORS: In production, lock allowed origins rather than using a permissive cors() without options. Or serve frontend and backend from the same origin.
- Authentication: Protect endpoints with tokens (e.g., JWT) and send Authorization header from the frontend.
- Validation & error handling: Validate request bodies on the server and return clear HTTP status codes.
- For larger apps consider using a client library (Axios) and a build tool / bundler for the frontend.

If you want, I can adapt the example to your tech stack (React / Vue, Django / Flask / Spring Boot) or add authentication. Which stack do you use?"]

